tel 1.0

base zephyrine
import soundness

info   the streaming kernel: cursors for parsers, pull and push endpoints, ducts, producers and the buffering policy
detail
    _Zephyrine_ is the layer every Soundness parser and serializer stands on. It supplies
    three things: a **`Cursor`**, the one-element-at-a-time view a parser reads through, over
    a single contiguous buffer which refills from its source however the source was chunked;
    the **streaming kernel** — a `Stream` is a pull endpoint, an `Intake` a push endpoint, a
    `Duct` a synchronous transformation attachable to either, and a `Conduit` the one place
    data crosses between threads — with demand (`Credit`) as the backpressure message; and a
    **`Producer`**, the sink a serializer writes into, which the same writing code drives
    either synchronously (`Producer.collect`) or as a stream of blocks (`Producer.apply`,
    `Producer.sink`, `Producer.utf8`).

    The format libraries parse over a `Cursor`: jacinta's JSON parser, stratiform's TEL
    parser, locomotion's Protobuf parser, breviloquence's CBOR parser, xylophone, ypsiloid
    and honeycomb each extend `Cursor.Snapshot`, keeping the cursor's buffer, read position
    and write end in their own fields for register-resident hot loops, and sync back to the
    cursor at every refill, mark and error. A protocol parser (telekinesis's request-head
    parser) uses the cursor directly, through `peek`, `expect`, `seek`, `hold`, `mark` and
    `grab`. On the writing side, those same formats render through a `Producer[Text]` or
    `Producer[Data]`, so `show`, `emit` and `lend` share one writer.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val cursor = Cursor(Iterator(t"hello ", t"world"))
    cursor.peek == 'h'        // true: the current character, without consuming it
    cursor.advance()          // past it, never touching the source
    cursor.position           // Sec: the second character

    val word: Text = cursor.hold:
      val start = cursor.mark
      while cursor.peek != ' ' do cursor.advance()
      cursor.grab(start, cursor.mark)   // t"ello", materialized across the two chunks
    ```

    A `Cursor` is built over a whole value (`Cursor(data)`, zero-copy for `Data`), an
    `Iterator` of chunks, a `Chain` of chunks, a `Stream`, or a loader thunk, and its medium
    is anything `Addressable`: `Data` (byte cursors), `Text` (char cursors) or a frozen array
    of records. The **blocking-lookahead hazard** governs every parser on a live connection:
    `more`, `finished`, `peek` and `next()` refill from the source once the buffer drains,
    and a refill blocks until the source yields; a parser which stops at a message boundary
    with no end-of-stream between messages must consume the final delimiter with `peek` (or
    `expect`) then `advance()`, which never refills, and bound its reads with `available`,
    `take` or an explicit length.

    Streams compose on the consumer's thread with no queues: `stream.via(stage)` attaches any
    value with a `Ductile` instance (a `Charset`, a `Codepage`, a compression scheme, a
    cipher mode), and a terminal — `memoize`, `drain`, `gather`, `chunks`, `records` — drains
    and closes it. A `Stream` is an exclusive capability: it is read once by one owner, and
    the modules which use it compile with capture and separation checking, so aliasing a
    stream or reading it after a consuming operation is a compile error.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import codepages.utf8Codepage
    import charsets.utf8Charset

    val bytes: Data = t"The quick brown fox".in[Data]
    val text: Text = bytes.stream.via(summon[Charset]).memoize
    bytes.stream.gather(0L): region => (count, range) => count + (range: Interval).size
    ```

    Every stage sizes its buffers through the `Buffering` given in scope (`Buffering.standard`
    unless another is provided): 4096 bytes, 2048 chars or 256 records per staging block,
    sixteen staging blocks per asynchronous transfer, and a conduit depth of sixteen.

    The library is one component, `zephyrine.core`, for the JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native,
    with the SWAR word read behind `Words.load` supplied per platform: `core-jvm` reads a
    word through a byte-array `VarHandle`, `core-portable` by shift-and-or. Everything in
    the export list is in the `soundness` umbrella, and the `lineation` and `parsing` choice
    packages merge with the umbrella's own. `Region` and `Slate` are deliberately *not*
    exported (their dependent-typed inline extensions do not survive export forwarders), nor
    are the kernel's internals `Utf8`, `Words`, `WordLoad`, `Blockpool`, `Handoff` and
    `Freelist`: these are reached as `zephyrine.Region`, `zephyrine.Utf8` and so on.

    Givens imported by name, since `import soundness.*` imports none of them: a `Lineation`
    which tracks line and column (`import lineation.linefeedChar` or `linefeedByte`; the
    companion defaults `Lineation.untrackedChars`/`untrackedData` track nothing), and
    `import parsing.trackPositions`, which makes the position-aware parsers record source
    positions for `locate`.

    Indexing discipline: a `Cursor`'s buffer is read through `lend`, which lends a `Region`
    with a branded `Interval in region.type` so every index a consumer can form is in range,
    or — for a trusted hot loop which snapshots the buffer into fields — through the
    `unsafeBuffer`/`unsafePos`/`unsafeWriteEnd` triple behind an `Unsafe` token, whose
    reference is valid only until the next operation which may refill, compact or grow the
    buffer. `readUnchecked` and `charAt` reads inside the kernel are each justified by a
    comment naming the invariant which keeps the index in range; consumer code should prefer
    the branded combinators.

refer zephyrine.Cursor
refer zephyrine.Stream
refer zephyrine.Intake
refer zephyrine.Duct
refer zephyrine.Producer
refer zephyrine.Buffering
refer zephyrine.Addressable

entry .Cursor   a position over a buffered sequence of bytes, chars or records, refilling from its source as it advances
  detail
      A `Cursor[data, cap]` reads one element at a time from a single contiguous buffer, which
      it refills from its source as the read position passes the end — so a parser sees one
      unbroken run of elements however the source was chunked. `data` is the medium (`Data`,
      `Text`, or a frozen `Array[record]^{}`) and `cap^` is the capture set of the loader:
      `{}` for a cursor over a value, an iterator, a chain or a stream, or the capabilities a
      streaming loader draws from, so a cursor over a file-reading loader cannot leave the
      scope of the file.

      A cursor is a stateful capability: a bare `Cursor` reference may only query `position`,
      `line`, `column` and `available`, while the exclusive `Cursor[…]^` which the factories
      return is needed for every navigating method (`advance`, `next`, `more`, `seek`,
      `hold`, `mark`, `cue`, `take`…). It is `caps.Mutable`, so a consumer may hold one in a
      field (a pooled parser does); a cursor built over an iterator or a stream is best used
      inline, as a `consume` argument, rather than bound and reused.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A cursor over two chunks of text
        code
            import soundness.*

            val cursor = Cursor(Iterator(t"name = ", t"Zephyrine"))
            cursor.peek == 'n'   // true: the current character, without consuming it
      frame
        label Advance to the delimiter, materializing the key
        code
            import soundness.*

            val cursor = Cursor(Iterator(t"name = ", t"Zephyrine"))

            val key: Text = cursor.hold:
              val start = cursor.mark
              while cursor.peek != ' ' do cursor.advance()
              cursor.grab(start, cursor.mark)   // t"name"
      frame
        label Take a snapshot, look ahead, and restore it
        code
            import soundness.*

            val cursor = Cursor(Iterator(t"name = ", t"Zephyrine"))

            val key: Text = cursor.hold:
              val start = cursor.mark
              while cursor.peek != ' ' do cursor.advance()
              cursor.grab(start, cursor.mark)   // t"name"

            val assigned: Boolean = cursor.lookahead:
              cursor.next() && cursor.peek == '='   // true, and the cursor is cued back to the space
      frame
        label Consume the separator and the value, across the chunk boundary
        code
            import soundness.*

            val cursor = Cursor(Iterator(t"name = ", t"Zephyrine"))

            val key: Text = cursor.hold:
              val start = cursor.mark
              while cursor.peek != ' ' do cursor.advance()
              cursor.grab(start, cursor.mark)   // t"name"

            val assigned: Boolean = cursor.lookahead:
              cursor.next() && cursor.peek == '='

            cursor.consume(())("= ")                 // steps over the separator, stopping on its last character
            cursor.advance()
            val value: Text = cursor.take(t"")(9)   // t"Zephyrine", refilled from the second chunk
            cursor.finished                          // true
      ##
      ```

      Marks may be taken only inside `#hold`, which pins the buffer from the hold's start so
      that compaction cannot discard a marked region; outside a hold the cursor is free to
      drop what it has passed. `#grab` materializes the elements between two marks as one
      value, `#clone` copies them into a builder, `#slice` lends the storage, and `#cue`
      returns to a mark.

      **Blocking-lookahead hazard.** Every operation which asks whether an element exists —
      `#more`, `#finished`, `.peek`, and `#next` (whose trailing `more` runs after consuming)
      — refills from the source once the buffer drains, and a refill blocks until the source
      yields data or ends. On finite input that is prompt and harmless; on a live connection
      with no end-of-stream between messages (HTTP keep-alive, any request/response
      protocol), a parser must never look past a message's final element, since the peer
      sends nothing more until it is answered. Consume the final delimiter with `.peek`
      then `#advance` (as `.expect` and `#take` do), and bound reads with `#available`,
      `#take` or an explicit length.

      Line and column are tracked only when the `Lineation` in scope is active:
      `import lineation.linefeedChar` (or `linefeedByte`) counts lines at `\n`; the companion
      defaults `Lineation.untrackedChars` and `untrackedData` track nothing and cost nothing
      per advance. A parser which runs a bulk scan and pushes the position back in one step
      (`#unsafeAdvanceBy`) is responsible for its own line and column updates
      (`#unsafeBumpLine`, `#unsafeBumpColumn`, `#unsafeSetColumn`).
  refer zephyrine.Cursor.Snapshot
  refer zephyrine.Datum
  refer zephyrine.Addressable
  refer zephyrine.Lineation
  refer zephyrine.streamOf
  entry .apply   a cursor over a value, an iterator or chain of chunks, a stream, or a loader thunk
      Five factories, each needing the medium's `Addressable` (in implicit scope for `Data`,
      `Text` and record arrays) and a `Lineation` for its element type (the untracked default
      resolves unless a tracking one is imported by name):

      - `Cursor(value)` — a pre-filled cursor over a whole value. A medium which exposes its
        backing storage (`Data`) is borrowed in place with no copy, and the cursor is
        *static*: it never writes its buffer, never pulls, and may be `#repoint`ed. Any other
        medium is copied once.
      - `Cursor(iterator)` — over a `murmuration.Iterator` of chunks, pulled one per refill.
        The cursor's `Operand` and `Target` members are abstract on this form, so a direct
        `clone` or `seek` casts its argument (`' '.toByte.asInstanceOf[cursor.addressable.Operand]`);
        the per-medium extensions `peek`, `expect` and the typed buffers are unaffected.
      - `Cursor(chain)` — over a lazy `Chain` of chunks, forcing at most one cell per refill.
      - `Cursor(stream)` — over a consumed `Stream[data] over Credit` (needs a `Buffering`).
        When nothing of its own is live the cursor reads the stream's next region in place,
        with no copy, with a credit of the `Buffering`'s `window`; while a hold spans a
        refill it transfers the region into its own buffer in one copy. The credit bounds what
        any upstream stage produces per fill, so memory stays bounded through a parse of an
        arbitrarily large input.
      - `Cursor(load)` — a transparent inline factory over a thunk `() ->{cap} Optional[data]`,
        yielding each chunk and `Unset` at the end; the thunk's capture set becomes the
        cursor's `cap`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      val bytes: Data = t"GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n".in[Data]
      val direct = Cursor(bytes)
      val chunked = Cursor(Iterator(t"one", t"two", t"three"))
      val streamed = Cursor[Text](Stream(Iterator(t"ab", t"cd")))
      ```

      Every cursor pre-loads its first non-empty chunk, so `unsafeDatum` and `peek` are valid
      before any `next()`.
  entry .Mark   an absolute position in the stream, taken inside a hold, to return to or copy from
    detail
        An opaque `Long`: the absolute stream offset at which `Cursor#mark` was called. Marks
        survive compaction, since they are stream positions rather than buffer indices, but a
        mark may be taken only inside `Cursor#hold`, which keeps its region buffered. Two
        marks bound a `grab`, a `clone` or a `slice`, and `cue` returns to one. `Mark.Initial`
        is position zero; `Mark(absolute)` makes one from an offset. Marks compare by offset.
        `Mark` is also exported at the package level, as `zephyrine.Mark`.
    entry .Initial   the mark at the start of the stream: offset zero
    entry .apply   a mark at an absolute stream offset
    entry .comparable   marks compare and sort by their absolute offset
  entry .Offset   a line and column packed into one `Long`, recorded for each mark when lineation is active
    detail
        `Offset(line, column)` packs two ordinals into a `Long`; `Cursor#offset(mark)` looks
        up the offset recorded when a mark was taken, so that `cue` restores the line and
        column along with the position. Offsets compare by their packed value. `Offset` is
        also exported at the package level, as `zephyrine.Offset`.
    entry .apply   an offset from a line and a column, both ordinals
    entry .comparable   offsets compare by their packed value
  entry .absolute   the absolute stream offset of a `Mark`, as a `Long`
  entry .line   the line of an `Offset`, as an ordinal
  entry .column   the column of an `Offset`, as an ordinal
  entry .Held   the witness that a hold is open, required by `mark`
      A stateless token: `Cursor#mark` takes a `using Cursor.Held`, which only `Cursor#hold`
      (and `.lookahead`) provide, so a mark can only be taken where compaction cannot drop
      the marked region. One shared instance, `.shared`, serves every hold.
  entry .shared   the one `Cursor.Held` instance every hold supplies
  entry .Loader   the loader thunk type: `() => Optional[data]`, a chunk per call and `Unset` at the end
  entry .Filler   a refill strategy writing straight into the cursor's buffer, bypassing the chunk protocol
    detail
        `fill(storage, offset, space)` writes up to `space` elements into the cursor's buffer
        at `offset` and returns the count written, `0` when nothing was granted (the cursor
        retries) or `-1` at end-of-stream, so a pull endpoint's region is transferred into
        the cursor in a single copy rather than materialized as a chunk and copied again.
        `block` is the growth hint: the cursor grows its buffer towards `writeEnd + block`
        before each fill. The stream factory `Cursor(stream)` installs one.
    entry #block   the growth hint: how much space the cursor makes before each fill
    entry #fill   writes up to `space` elements at `offset`, returning the count, `0` or `-1`
  entry .Lender   a filler whose source can also lend a region to be read in place, with no copy
    detail
        `borrow()` exposes the source's next region — `lentStorage`, `lentStart` and the
        returned count (`0` if nothing was granted, `-1` at the end) — which the cursor reads
        in place whenever nothing in its own buffer is live. The region stays valid until the
        next `borrow` or `fill`, so a source which recycles its blocks never overwrites one
        the cursor is still reading.
    entry #borrow   lends the source's next region, returning its count, `0` or `-1`
    entry #lentStorage   the storage of the region last lent
    entry #lentStart   the start of the region last lent, within its storage
  entry .Delimiter   a byte sequence to be found in bulk, with its Horspool skip table computed once
    detail
        A multipart boundary, an archive signature, a line terminator: `Cursor.Delimiter(bytes)`
        builds the shift table once, and `#find` probes the last byte of each candidate
        window, shifting by the table, so on data unlike the delimiter it visits about one
        byte in `length` instead of every byte. A `Cursor[Data]` searches its buffer for one
        with `.distance`, and `streamOf(cursor, delimiter)` lends everything before the next
        occurrence as a stream.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import codepages.utf8Codepage

        val boundary = Cursor.Delimiter(t"\r\n--frontier".in[Data])
        val cursor = Cursor(t"part one\r\n--frontier\r\npart two".in[Data])
        cursor.distance(boundary)   // 8
        ```
    entry .apply   a delimiter over a byte sequence, building its skip table
    entry #bytes   the delimiter's bytes
    entry #length   the delimiter's length in bytes
    entry #find   the index at which the delimiter first begins wholly within `from` until `end`, or `-1`
  entry .Snapshot   the parser-local copy of a cursor's buffer, position and write end, as an inline trait
    detail
        A fast parser does not read every element through the cursor: it keeps its own copy
        of the buffer reference (`cells`, a real `byte[]` or `char[]`), the read position
        (`pos`) and the write end (`bufEnd`) in plain fields the JIT keeps in registers, and
        syncs with the cursor only at refill, mark, slice and error points. `Snapshot[medium,
        operand]` is that protocol as an inline trait: its body is copied into each parser
        with `operand` substituted, so `Snapshot[Data, Byte]` gives a `byte[]` field and
        `Snapshot[Text, Char]` a `char[]` one — a specialisation an ordinary trait cannot
        express. The parser is the receiver (`this` is the parser), it needs no language
        flag, and the cursor is held as a neutral `AnyRef` (`cursor0`) with an exclusive-view
        accessor, so a pooled parser can re-point it at each reset.

        The protected protocol a parser follows: assign `cursor0` and call `syncFrom()` to
        start; read with `peek` and `advance()` while `more` holds; call `syncTo()` before any
        cursor operation which consults the position and `syncFrom()` after any which may
        compact, grow or adopt a buffer. Two hooks are abstract and **must** be implemented:
        `reconcile()` (called before every refill, where a position-tracking parser catches
        the cursor's lineation up with `reconcileLineation()`, `reconcileUtf8()` or
        `reconcileUtf16()`; otherwise `()`) and `resynced()` (called after every `syncFrom()`,
        for a parser which derives anything from the buffer's scale; otherwise `()`). They
        are abstract deliberately: the copied body's calls to a member the receiver
        *overrides* bind to the trait's own copy, not the override, so a hook with a default
        body would never reach the parser's version. Overriding any concrete member of the
        trait silently misbehaves for the same reason.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import scala.caps

        final class Counter extends caps.ExclusiveCapability, caps.Stateful, Cursor.Snapshot[Data, Byte]:
          protected update def reconcile(): Unit = ()
          protected update def resynced(): Unit = ()

          update def count(input: Data): Int =
            cursor0 = Cursor(input).asInstanceOf[AnyRef]
            syncFrom()
            var commas = 0

            while more do
              if peek == ','.toByte then commas += 1
              advance()

            commas
        ```

        A snapshot may stand over a whole input with no cursor at all (`cells` the input's
        own array, `cursor0` null); then the input ends at `bufEnd`. jacinta, xylophone,
        stratiform, ypsiloid, honeycomb, locomotion and breviloquence each extend it.
    entry #position   the absolute stream offset of the next element: what every error reports
        `base + pos`, where `base` is re-anchored by every `syncFrom()` so the position
        survives compaction. Public, as `#more` is: a parser's driver asks both of a finished
        parse.
    entry #more   whether another element is available, refilling from the source if the buffer is exhausted
        `pos < bufEnd || moreSlow()`: the slow path syncs to the cursor, calls the
        `reconcile()` hook, asks the cursor for more and syncs back, so the JIT keeps the
        fast path as one register comparison. Subject to the blocking-lookahead hazard
        described on `Cursor`.
  entry .peek   the current element as a `Datum`, or `Datum.End` when the cursor is finished
      An extension on `Cursor[data, cap]` for byte and char cursors (any other medium is a
      compile error). Allocation-free: a byte is lifted unsigned (`0..255`) and a char by its
      code, so the result compares directly with a `Char` or `Byte` literal and the comparison
      compiles to one `int == int`. It may block to fetch the element being inspected (that
      element must exist for any match) but never reads past it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val cursor = Cursor(t"{}")
      cursor.peek == '{'           // true
      cursor.advance()
      cursor.advance()
      cursor.peek == Datum.End     // true
      ```
  entry .expect   matches the current element against a char, advancing past it or raising the given error
      An extension on byte and char cursors. `cursor.expect('H')(Mismatch())` raises
      `failure` through the `Tactic[error]` in scope on a mismatch or at end-of-stream, and
      otherwise consumes the element with `advance()` — never `next()` — so consuming the
      final delimiter of a message on a keep-alive stream does not force a blocking refill
      for the byte after it. The target is a `Char` for both media, so a `Cursor[Data]` needs
      no `.toByte`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Mismatch()(using Diagnostics) extends Error(m"mismatch")

      val cursor = Cursor(t"HTTP/1.1".in[Data])
      cursor.expect('H')(Mismatch())
      cursor.expect('T')(Mismatch())
      cursor.peek == 'T'   // true
      ```
  entry .lookahead   runs an action inside a hold and restores the cursor to where it was on entry
      An extension on any cursor: a non-consuming lookahead. Marks taken inside the action
      are available through the implicit `Cursor.Held`, and the cursor is cued back on exit
      whatever the result, so subsequent parsing sees the same elements. Inside an outer
      hold, the outer marks are preserved.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val cursor = Cursor(t"abcd")
      cursor.lookahead(cursor.next() && cursor.peek == 'b')   // true
      cursor.peek == 'a'                                      // still true
      ```
  entry .unsafeDataBuffer   the current backing storage of a `Cursor[Data]`, as a `scala.Array[Byte]`
      The typed form of `#unsafeBuffer`, behind an `Unsafe` token: the reference is valid
      only until the next cursor operation which may compact or grow the buffer, and the
      caller takes responsibility for indexing it within `unsafePos` until `unsafeWriteEnd`.
  entry .distance   how far ahead of a `Cursor[Data]` a `Cursor.Delimiter` first begins within the buffer, or `-1`
      Searches the buffered region in bulk; the cursor is not moved and nothing is refilled,
      so unlike `#seek` this cannot block on a live stream. A scan which finds nothing should
      consume all but the last `delimiter.length - 1` buffered bytes (a possible prefix of a
      delimiter straddling the refill) and search again once more has arrived — which is
      what `streamOf(cursor, delimiter)` does.
  entry .unsafeTextBuffer   the current backing storage of a `Cursor[Text]`, as a `scala.Array[Char]`
      As `.unsafeDataBuffer`, for char cursors.
  entry #addressable   the medium's `Addressable` instance, a tracked value naming its `Storage`, `Operand` and `Target`
  entry #lineation   the `Lineation` the cursor was built with
  entry #repoint   re-points a static cursor at another borrowed region, resetting every position
      Only a cursor built with `Cursor(value)` over a medium with an exposed backing (`Data`)
      may be re-pointed; any other panics. It is the one-cursor-per-connection idiom: a
      server parses each request head straight out of its accumulator without allocating a
      cursor or copying the head out. The storage is the same neutral carrier the factory
      takes (`storage: AnyRef`, a `scala.Array[Byte]` for `Data`), and the region must stay
      unwritten until the parse which reads it has finished.
  entry #advance   consumes the current element without touching the source
      Increments the position (and the line or column, when lineation is active). It is
      unchecked: the position may sit one past the buffer's end, since the next `more`,
      `peek` or `next()` forces a refill before the buffer is read. It is therefore the
      boundary-safe primitive: the only way to consume an element *without* a possible
      refill, which is what a parser stopping at a message boundary on a live stream must
      use for the final delimiter (as `.expect` and `#take` do).
  entry #unsafeAdvanceWith   advances past an element already read, reusing it for lineation tracking
      For a scan loop which has just read the current element from `#unsafeBuffer`: the
      supplied operand feeds line and column tracking instead of being re-loaded.
  entry #unsafeBumpPos   advances the position by `by` elements with no lineation update
      A bulk-advance primitive for register-resident scan loops: the caller tracks newlines
      itself and reconciles lineation in one step with `#unsafeBumpLine`,
      `#unsafeBumpColumn` and `#unsafeSetColumn`.
  entry #unsafeBumpLine   increases the line counter by `by`, leaving the column untouched
  entry #unsafeBumpColumn   increases the column counter by `by`; must not be called across a newline
  entry #unsafeSetColumn   sets the column directly, after a bulk advance over a range containing a newline
  entry #next   advances and reports whether more remains: `advance(); more`
      Returns `true` while elements remain and `false` once the source is exhausted. Its
      trailing `more` blocks on a refill once the buffer drains, so it must never consume the
      last element of a message on a live stream; use `peek` then `advance()` there.
  entry #more   whether an element is available at the current position, refilling the buffer if it has drained
      The hot path is one comparison, `pos < writeEnd`; only when the buffer is drained does
      the out-of-line slow path refill. Blocks until the source yields data or ends.
  entry #finished   whether the source is exhausted: `!more`
  entry #position   the absolute stream position of the next element, as an ordinal
  entry #available   how many elements are buffered beyond the current position, without refilling
      The bound a live-stream parser reads within, since reading past it would refill.
  entry #line   the current line, as an ordinal, when the `Lineation` tracks lines; `Prim` otherwise
  entry #column   the current column, as an ordinal, when the `Lineation` tracks lines; `Prim` otherwise
  entry #lineActive   whether the configured `Lineation` is active, cached at construction
      Hot-loop callers test this rather than `lineation.active`, which would be an interface
      dispatch per element.
  entry #remainder   a lazy `Chain` of everything unconsumed from the current position onwards
      The buffered tail first, as one chunk, then the loader's chunks as they are pulled.
      Caller-driven: construction pulls nothing, so a protocol which withholds data until it
      gets a reply (a WebSocket upgrade) is not deadlocked by building the chain. The cursor's
      position moves to the end of its buffer.
  entry #unsafeBuffer   the raw backing storage, behind `Unsafe`
      The `addressable.Storage` the cursor is currently reading, which may be its own buffer
      or a region its source lent. Valid only until the next operation which may refill,
      compact or grow. A parser which snapshots it into a field casts it once to the concrete
      array type (`Cursor.Snapshot` does this), so the JIT keeps the reference in a register;
      reading through `addressable.storageAddress` instead costs an interface call and an
      unbox per element. The typed forms are `.unsafeDataBuffer` and `.unsafeTextBuffer`.
  entry #unsafePos   the read index into the buffer, behind `Unsafe`
  entry #unsafeWriteEnd   the count of valid elements in the buffer, behind `Unsafe`
  entry #lend   lends the readable region, with a branded interval, to a lambda
      The bounds-safe view of elements `pos until writeEnd`: the lambda receives a
      `Region[data]` and an `Interval in region.type`, so every index it can form is in
      range, with no `Unsafe`. Valid only for the duration of the call. The preferred
      boundary for a scan loop which does not cache the buffer across cursor operations.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val cursor = Cursor(t"abc")
      val count: Int = cursor.lend: region => range => (range: Interval).size   // 3
      cursor.unsafeAdvanceBy(count)(using Unsafe)
      ```
  entry #unsafeAdvanceBy   advances the position by `n` elements with no lineation tracking
      For a parser maintaining its own copy of the position: push it back to the cursor at
      refill, mark or slice points. The caller is responsible for line and column updates
      when `#lineActive` holds.
  entry #unsafeDatum   the current element, read through the `Addressable` instance, behind `Unsafe`
      Valid only when the cursor is not `finished`; reading at the end sees stale storage.
      `peek` is the safe form.
  entry #lay   the lambda applied to the current element, or `otherwise` when the cursor is finished
  entry #let   runs a lambda on the current element, if there is one
  entry #process   runs a lambda on the current element, if there is one, discarding its result
  entry #seek   advances to the next element equal to the target, reporting whether it was found
      Stops *on* the match, without consuming it, and performs no refill after finding it.
      The target is the medium's `Operand` (so a caller holding a cursor from the iterator
      factory writes `' '.toByte.asInstanceOf[cursor.addressable.Operand]`); it is unboxed
      once and compared at the primitive type, and a byte matches the ASCII char of the same
      value. On a live stream a search which does not find its target refills until the
      source ends.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      val cursor = Cursor(t"key=value".in[Data])
      cursor.seek('='.toByte.asInstanceOf[cursor.addressable.Operand])   // true
      cursor.peek == '='                                                   // true: stopped on it
      ```
  entry #consume   matches a literal string element by element, running `otherwise` on the first mismatch
      A macro: `cursor.consume(fail())("--")` expands to one `next()` and comparison per
      character of the literal, which must be a constant. Each character is compared *after*
      a `next()`, so the cursor must stand on the element before the literal when it is
      called, and stands on the literal's last character afterwards. Each step uses `next()`,
      so it is not safe for the final bytes of a message on a live stream.
  entry #mark   the current position as a `Cursor.Mark`, taken inside a hold
      Needs a `Cursor.Held`, which only `#hold` and `.lookahead` provide. When lineation is
      active the line and column are recorded beside the mark, in unboxed parallel arrays,
      so `#cue` can restore them.
  entry #offset   the line and column recorded when a mark was taken
  entry #cue   returns the cursor to a mark, restoring its line and column
      The mark must lie within the current hold, since only a held region is guaranteed to
      still be buffered.
  entry #hold   runs an action with the buffer pinned from the current position, so marks may be taken
      From the start of the outermost hold until it ends, compaction may not discard anything
      after the hold's start, so every mark taken inside is still buffered; nested holds
      share the outer pin. Holds are closed, and marks released, when the outermost returns.
      A hold spanning a very long region keeps all of it in memory.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val cursor = Cursor(Iterator(t"one", t"two", t"three"))

      cursor.hold:
        val start = cursor.mark
        for i <- 1 to 7 do cursor.next()
        cursor.grab(start, cursor.mark)   // t"onetwot"
      ```
  entry #grab   the elements between two marks, materialized as one value of the medium
      An empty value when the marks coincide or are reversed. Both marks must lie in the
      current hold.
  entry #slice   lends the storage, offset and length between two marks to a lambda, zero-copy
      The lambda must not retain the storage reference, which compaction may invalidate after
      the call.
  entry #clone   copies the elements between two marks into a builder of the medium's `Target` type
      For `Text` the target is a `java.lang.StringBuilder`, for `Data` a `ByteArrayOutputStream`.
  entry #take   the next `length` elements as one value, or `otherwise` if the source ends first
      Makes each element present before consuming it and never refills after the last, so it
      is safe for the final bytes of a message on a live stream (a WebSocket frame's mask
      then payload, arriving a byte per chunk). If the stream ends early, yields `otherwise`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val cursor = Cursor(Data.fill(20)(_.toByte): Data)
      cursor.take(Data())(10)   // the first ten bytes
      ```

entry .Datum   a byte or char read by `peek`, or `Datum.End`, comparing directly with a `Char` or `Byte` literal
  detail
      An opaque `Int`, exported from `internal`: an unsigned byte (`0..255`), a char's code,
      or the sentinel `Datum.End` (`-1`) for an exhausted cursor — the only datum not
      derivable from a byte or char. It is distinct from a raw `Int` so that arithmetic or
      byte/char confusion cannot happen silently; comparison with a literal goes through the
      dedicated `CanEqual` givens and still compiles to a single `int == int`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Datum('-'.toByte) == '-'.toByte   // true
      Datum(0xff.toByte).asInt          // 255
      Datum.End.isEnd                   // true
      Datum.End == 0.toByte             // false
      ```
  refer zephyrine.Cursor.peek

entry .Credit   fine-grained backpressure demand: how many more elements a consumer can accept
  detail
      An opaque `Long` (`<: Long`, so capture checking treats it as an untracked value),
      exported from `internal`: a count of elements of the medium's `Operand` type — bytes,
      chars or records — not a byte count, so its meaning changes across a `Duct` which
      changes medium, and `Duct#translate` performs that conversion. `Credit(Long.MaxValue)`
      is unbounded demand. It is the `Transport` of every endpoint the kernel's factories
      build (`Stream[Data] over Credit`), and `Regulation.credit` is its arithmetic.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val stream = t"hello".stream
      stream.refill(Credit(3))   // 3: three characters readable
      ```
  refer zephyrine.Regulation
  refer zephyrine.Pace

entry .Mark   `Cursor.Mark`, exported at the package level
    The opaque mark type is `export`ed from the `Cursor` companion, so it is reachable as
    `zephyrine.Mark` as well as `Cursor.Mark`; see `Cursor.Mark`.

entry .Offset   `Cursor.Offset`, exported at the package level
    The packed line-and-column type is `export`ed from the `Cursor` companion, so it is
    reachable as `zephyrine.Offset` as well as `Cursor.Offset`; see `Cursor.Offset`.

entry .Addressable   the typeclass of a streaming medium: its element, its storage, its builder, and the copies between them
  detail
      `medium is Addressable` is what lets a `Cursor`, a `Stream`, an `Intake`, a `Duct` or a
      `Producer` be generic over `Data`, `Text` and chunks of records. It names three types —
      `Operand`, the element (`Byte`, `Char` or the record type); `Storage`, the mutable
      buffer (`scala.Array[Byte]`, `scala.Array[Char]` or an erased `scala.Array[AnyRef]`);
      and `Target`, the builder (`ByteArrayOutputStream`, `StringBuilder`, `ArrayBuffer`) —
      and the primitives which move elements between a value, its storage and a builder. The
      instances are in the companion, so they resolve with no import: `.bytes` for `Data`,
      `.text` for `Text`, `.texts` for `Array[Text]^{}`, `.frames` for `Array[Data]^{}` and
      `.boxed` for any other `Array[record]^{}` with a `ClassTag`. The `Data` and `Text`
      instances are `inline given`s, so a hot-path call site which resolves one at compile
      time lowers to a direct array access.

      A medium names its type exactly: a branded `Data & Sized[16]` is not a medium, so a
      value meant for `Cursor(…)`, `Stream(…)` or `stream` is ascribed `: Data`.

      The storage protocol is capture-typed: `allocate` mints a fresh exclusive buffer,
      writers take it as `Storage^`, and readers as a read-only view. A consumer of the
      kernel rarely calls these directly — `Region`, `Slate` and the endpoints wrap them —
      but an implementation of a `Stream`, an `Intake` or a `Duct` does.
  refer zephyrine.Substrate
  refer zephyrine.Region
  entry .bytes   `Data is Addressable`: bytes in a `scala.Array[Byte]`, built by a `ByteArrayOutputStream`
      Exposes its backing array (`#backing`), so a `Data` chunk is lent to a cursor, a stream
      or a conduit by reference, never copied, and `assemble` joins pieces with one copy.
  entry .text   `Text is Addressable`: chars in a `scala.Array[Char]`, built by a `java.lang.StringBuilder`
      A `String` is not a `char[]`, so a text chunk has no exposable backing and is copied into
      a buffer; `assemble` joins pieces through `String.join`, sizing the result exactly.
  entry .boxed   `Array[element]^{} is Addressable` for any reference element type with a `ClassTag`
      The record medium: a chunk is a frozen array of records, the working storage an erased
      `scala.Array[AnyRef]`, and credit counts records (`Substrate.Boxes`). The element must
      erase to a reference type, and the `ClassTag` makes the materialized chunks genuine
      `element[]`s. `Text` and `Data` elements, which are not `<: AnyRef`, have the dedicated
      instances `.texts` and `.frames`.
  entry .texts   `Array[Text]^{} is Addressable`: chunks of text records (lines, tokens)
  entry .frames   `Array[Data]^{} is Addressable`: chunks of byte records (frames, messages)
  entry #Storage   the mutable buffer type: `scala.Array[Byte]`, `scala.Array[Char]` or `scala.Array[AnyRef]`
  entry #empty   the empty value of the medium
  entry #substrate   how the storage is physically represented, for `Buffering` to size buffers
  entry #blank   a fresh builder, sized by a hint
  entry #build   the value a builder holds
  entry #length   the number of elements in a value
  entry #address   the element at an ordinal of a value
  entry #clone   copies the elements of a value between two ordinals, inclusive, into a builder
  entry #grab   the elements of a value between two ordinals, as a new value
  entry #allocate   mints a fresh, exclusive buffer of `size` elements
  entry #storageSize   the capacity of a buffer
  entry #storageAddress   the element at an index of a buffer
      Erases to an interface call returning `Object` plus an unbox when the instance is not
      resolved at compile time, which is why hot loops cast the buffer once instead.
  entry #storageUpdate   writes one element into an exclusive buffer at an index
  entry #append   appends one element to a builder
  entry #copyChunk   copies `len` elements of a value, from an offset, into a buffer at an offset
  entry #transfer   copies `len` elements from one buffer to another
  entry #materialize   a new value of `len` elements copied from a buffer at an offset
  entry #cloneStorage   copies `len` elements of a buffer at an offset into a builder
  entry #assemble   joins exact-size pieces, in order, into one value of their total length
      The terminal of `memoize`: the default copies through fresh storage and `materialize`;
      `Data` and `Text` override it to copy once.
  entry #backing   the value's own storage, when the medium is immutable and its representation is its `Storage`
      `Data` returns its array; `Text` returns `Unset`, and callers copy. An exposed backing
      must never be written through: a cursor built over one is static, and a stream lending
      one reports `regionStable`.

entry .Substrate   how a buffer is physically represented: `Bytes`, `Chars` or `Boxes`
    `Bytes` and `Chars` have deterministic footprints; `Boxes` (buffers of heap records) are
    counted in references, so their memory use is not deterministically bounded, and
    `Buffering.standard` sizes them smaller (256 per staging block against 4096 bytes or 2048
    chars). An `Addressable` instance names its substrate.

entry .Buffering   the contextual sizing policy every stage consults when it allocates a buffer
  detail
      A `Buffering` is pure sizing policy — it captures nothing — consulted once per stage at
      construction, so an adaptive instance sees each new stage but never resizes a live one.
      `.standard` is in implicit scope; a pipeline is retuned by binding another as a local
      given. Its members: `#capacity(substrate)`, the staging block (cache-resident: 4096
      bytes, 2048 chars, 256 records); `#transfer(substrate)`, a block crossing an
      asynchronous boundary (sixteen staging blocks, since each costs a synchronized
      hand-off); `#depth`, the transfer blocks of headroom a `Conduit` keeps between writer
      and reader (16); `#window(substrate)`, the credit a cursor asks of a stream it reads in
      place (one staging block); and `#recycle`, whether a conduit's drained blocks return to
      its writer (on).

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given Buffering:
        def capacity(substrate: Substrate): Int = 64
        def depth: Int = 4
        override def transfer(substrate: Substrate): Int = 256
      ```

      A conduit's in-flight data is bounded by about `2*depth + 1` transfer blocks; hand-off
      throughput collapses at very shallow depths and plateaus around the default for a
      producer and consumer in lockstep, so a burst-heavy pipeline overrides `depth` to its
      burst size in transfer blocks.
  refer zephyrine.Conduit
  refer zephyrine.Substrate
  entry .standard   the default policy: 4096 bytes, 2048 chars or 256 records per block, depth 16
  entry #capacity   the staging block size for a substrate: what a stage allocates for its own buffer
  entry #depth   the number of transfer blocks a `Conduit` holds between its writer and reader
  entry #transfer   the size of a block crossing an asynchronous boundary; sixteen staging blocks by default
  entry #window   the credit a cursor asks of a stream it reads in place; one staging block by default
      A borrowed region costs no buffer, so a larger window means only fewer fills and longer
      bulk scans between them, bounded by what the credit lets an upstream stage produce.
  entry #recycle   whether a `Conduit` recycles drained transfer blocks back to its writer; on by default
      Off restores strict per-block allocation, or isolates the pool's effect in a
      measurement.

entry .Lineation   the policy deciding which element ends a line, so a cursor can track line and column
  detail
      `Lineation by Operand` has `active`, whether tracking is on at all, and `track(datum)`,
      whether the element ends a line. A `Cursor` reads `active` once at construction, so an
      inactive lineation costs nothing per advance. The companion's `.untrackedChars` and
      `.untrackedData` are the defaults in implicit scope, tracking nothing; the tracking
      policies live in the `lineation` choice package and are imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import lineation.linefeedChar

      val cursor = Cursor(t"one\ntwo")
      for i <- 1 to 4 do cursor.next()
      (cursor.line, cursor.column)   // (Sec, Prim)
      ```
  refer zephyrine.lineation
  entry .untrackedChars   the default for char cursors: no line or column tracking
  entry .untrackedData   the default for byte cursors: no line or column tracking
  entry #active   whether this policy tracks lines at all
  entry #track   whether an element ends a line

entry .lineation   the line-tracking policies, each a given to import by name
  detail
      `import soundness.*` brings none of these in: the untracked companion defaults resolve
      unless one is imported by name, and a lexically imported given outranks the companion's.
      Each is an `inline given`, so the per-element test inlines into the cursor's `advance`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import lineation.linefeedByte

      val cursor = Cursor(t"a\nb\nc".in[Data])
      while cursor.next() do ()
      cursor.line   // Ter
      ```
  entry .linefeedChar   a char cursor's line ends at `\n`
  entry .carriageReturnChar   a char cursor's line ends at `\r`
  entry .linefeedByte   a byte cursor's line ends at byte 10
  entry .carriageReturnByte   a byte cursor's line ends at byte 13

entry .PositionTracking   the toggle, shared by every position-aware parser, for recording source positions
  detail
      `On` or `Off`. The companion's `.default` is `Off`, so the generic `read`/`load` entry
      points of jacinta, stratiform, xylophone, ypsiloid and honeycomb parse without tracking
      and pay no cost; `import parsing.trackPositions` overrides it with `On`, and the same
      `read` then yields a value which `locate` can resolve to a source position. (Named
      `PositionTracking` rather than `Tracking` so as not to clash with contingency's
      `Tracking` under `import soundness.*`.)
  refer zephyrine.parsing
  refer zephyrine.Positionable
  entry .default   the companion default: `Off`

entry .parsing   the given which switches position tracking on, imported by name
  detail
      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import parsing.trackPositions

      summon[PositionTracking] == PositionTracking.On   // true
      ```
  entry .trackPositions   `PositionTracking.On`: the position-aware parsers record source positions

entry .Positionable   links a parsed value to the path type which addresses its nodes and the position each resolves to
  detail
      `Json is Positionable by JsonPointer to Json.Ast.Position`, for example: `Operand` is
      the path type, `Result` the concrete `Format.Position`. The uniform `locate` and
      `locateKey` extensions dispatch through it, so every tracking-aware format exposes
      position lookup the same way. A value parsed without `parsing.trackPositions` in scope
      carries no positions, and every lookup is `Unset`.
  entry #Result   the position type the format resolves to, a `Format.Position`
  entry #locate   the position of the node a path addresses, or `Unset`
  entry #locateKey   the position of the key matching the path's final segment, or `Unset`

entry .locate   the source position of the node a path addresses within a parsed value, or `Unset`
    An extension on any value with a `Positionable` instance; the path type is fixed by that
    instance (a `Json` is located by a `JsonPointer`). `Unset` when the path does not resolve,
    or when the value was parsed without position tracking.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import parsing.trackPositions
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import codepages.utf8Codepage

    val json = t"""{"name": "zephyrine"}""".read[Json]
    json.locate(JsonPointer / "name").let(_.describe)
    ```

entry .locateKey   the source position of the object or mapping key a path's final segment names, or `Unset`
    As `locate`, but for the key rather than its value.

entry .Format   a parsed format's name and the position and issue types its parse errors carry
  detail
      Each format object (jacinta's `Json`, stratiform's `Tel`, …) is a `Format`, naming its
      `Position` (where in the source) and `Issue` (what was wrong) types so that `Parse.Error`
      can report `the JSON was not valid at line 3, column 7 because …` uniformly.
  refer zephyrine.Parse
  entry .Position   where in the source an error was found
    detail
        `describe` renders the position; `offset` and `length`, both `Unset` by default, give a
        character range; and `span` is the uniform `Span` the public error API exposes, built
        from the offset and length unless a line-tracking implementor overrides it.
    entry #describe   the position, rendered as text
    entry #offset   the character offset, if known
    entry #length   the length of the affected range, if known
    entry #span   the position as a `Span`
  entry .Issue   what was wrong with the input
    entry #describe   the issue, as a message
  entry #name   the format's name, as it reads in an error message
  entry #Position   the format's position type
  entry #Issue   the format's issue type

entry .Parse   the error every format parser raises
  detail
      `Parse.Error(format, position, issue)` is error 594, _the `name` was not valid at
      `position` because `issue`_, with the format's own position and issue types; `#span`
      exposes the position as a `Span`. Raised through a `Tactic[Parse.Error]` by every
      parser built on the kernel.
  entry .Error   error 594: _the `format` was not valid at `position` because `issue`_
    entry #format   the format which rejected the input
    entry #position   where the input was rejected
    entry #issue   why it was rejected
    entry #span   the position as a uniform `Span`

entry .Formatting   the marker trait of a serializer's output-formatting configuration
    Indentation, line wrapping, trailing newlines and the like. Each format defines its own
    subtype with the parameters which suit it (YAML forbids tab indentation; HTML whitespace
    is structural) and exposes importable instances in its `formatting` namespace.

entry .Truncation   a stream which ended before it had delivered everything
  detail
      Named for the state of the data rather than for a raiser: a `Spring`'s pull and
      turbulence's `Streamable`, `Writable` and `Sink` all report it, so it sits with
      `Stream` and `Spring`. turbulence re-exports it, so `Truncation` resolves through
      `import soundness.*` and `import turbulence.*` alike.
  entry .Error   error 264: _the stream was cut prematurely after `total`_
    entry #total   how much had been delivered when the stream was cut

entry .Expanse   a random-access view of an expanse of bytes, for formats whose access is positional
  detail
      ZIP central directories, PDF cross-reference tables and memory-mapped files are read by
      offset, not sequentially; an `Expanse` is `size` and `read(offset, length)` over any
      medium which can serve ranged reads — a file channel, an in-memory buffer, an HTTP
      resource accepting `Range` requests. An implementation states its own lifetime
      discipline (holding a resource open for a scope, or re-acquiring it per read).
      `streamOf(expanse, offset, length)` reads a range of it as a stream, a transfer block
      at a time.
  refer zephyrine.Malleable
  refer zephyrine.streamOf
  entry #size   the expanse's length in bytes
  entry #read   `length` bytes from `offset`, as `Data`

entry .Malleable   an `Expanse` whose bytes may also be replaced in place, as a memory-mapped file's
  detail
      The region is fixed: `write` may not extend beyond `size`.
  entry #write   replaces the bytes at `offset` with `data`

entry .Spring   a re-materializable source of streams: each pull mints a fresh, exclusive endpoint over the same content
  detail
      A `Stream` is read once; a `Spring[medium]` can be pulled any number of times, each
      `apply()` giving a new stream over the same content. It is a trait rather than a
      function type because a lambda may not mint a fresh scoped capability as its result,
      where a method's result is re-leveled at each call site; and since `apply` takes a
      `Tactic[Truncation.Error]` (the pull is where a source cut short is reported), it is not
      a single abstract method, so a spring is written as an anonymous class.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      val payload: Data = t"body".in[Data]

      val spring: Spring[Data] = new Spring[Data]:
        def apply()(using Tactic[Truncation.Error]): (Stream[Data] over Credit)^ = payload.stream
      ```
  entry #apply   a fresh stream over the content, raising `Truncation.Error` if it is cut short

entry .Stream   the pull endpoint of a pipeline: a buffer whose readable region is lent zero-copy to one consumer
  detail
      A `Stream[medium] over transport` is read by calling `#refill(demand)` with the amount
      the consumer is prepared to take, reading the region it reports (`#lend`, or
      `#unsafeStorage` from `#start` to `#limit`), and `#skip`ping past what it has read.
      Nothing is copied and nothing is retained; a consumer which does not refill demands
      nothing, which is what makes backpressure the absence of a call. The `transport` is the
      demand type, `Credit` for everything the kernel's factories build.

      A stream is an exclusive capability: the `Stream[…]^` a factory returns is required to
      refill, skip or close, it is passed to the combinators as a `consume` argument, and
      using it after `via`, `pump`, `memoize` or any other consuming operation is a compile
      error. Streams are built with `.apply` over a value, a chain or a chunk iterator, with
      the `stream` extension, by `Conduit`, by `streamOf` over a cursor or an expanse, and by
      turbulence's `Streamable` instances; they are transformed with `via`/`viaDuct`,
      bounded with `truncate`/`discard`, connected to an intake with `pump`, and drained by a
      terminal — `memoize`, `drain`, `gather`, `chunks`, `chain`, `records` — which closes
      them.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val stream = Stream(Iterator(t"ab", t"cd"))

      stream.refill(Credit(10))                              // 2: one chunk at a time
      val first: Text = stream.lend(region => range => region.materialize(range))
      stream.skip(2)
      stream.refill(Credit(10))                              // 2 again, the second chunk
      stream.close()
      ```

      Implementing one: provide `storage0` (the buffer, as `AnyRef`), `start`, `limit`,
      `skip` and `refill`, and override `close` to release the source; the test suite's
      `Recorder`, which wraps another stream and records the demands it sees, is the
      smallest shape. A `Stream` extends `ExclusiveCapability, Stateful` rather than
      `Mutable` so that an implementation may capture its source (an iterator, a queue, a
      socket); the price is that a stream held in an exclusive field is read-only, so a
      wrapper holds its upstream through a neutral `AnyRef` carrier and re-asserts
      ownership in an accessor.
  refer zephyrine.Intake
  refer zephyrine.Duct
  refer zephyrine.Conduit
  refer zephyrine.memoize
  refer zephyrine.via
  entry .apply   a stream over a single value, a lazy chain of chunks, or a chunk iterator
      `Stream(value)` is a single-region in-memory stream: a `Data` is shared by reference
      (its region ranges are stable, so a fan-out may share them), and any other medium is
      copied once on first refill. `Stream(chain)` forces at most one cell per refill, so
      demand drives production exactly as far as the chain's laziness allows.
      `Stream(iterator)` adapts a `murmuration.Iterator` of chunks, exposing each chunk
      per refill as far as the demand grants; the iterator's own production is outside the
      stream's control, and its captures are retained alongside the stream's own.
  entry #addressable   the medium's `Addressable` instance
  entry #Transport   the demand type: `Credit` for everything the kernel builds
  entry #refill   ensures at least one element is readable, producing no more than `demand` permits, and returns how many
      Blocks if necessary. Returns `0` only when `demand` grants nothing, `Unset` at
      end-of-stream, and otherwise `limit - start`. Elements still unconsumed in the region
      are reported without producing more: an unskipped region is reported, not extended.
  entry #lend   lends the readable region and its branded interval to a lambda, zero-copy
      The lambda receives a `Region[medium]` and an `Interval in region.type` covering
      `start until limit`, so every index it can form is in range. Valid only until the next
      `refill` or `close`.
  entry #unsafeStorage   the raw backing storage, behind `Unsafe`
      For fan-out plumbing which shares region ranges by reference, and for kernels whose
      index arithmetic outruns the region combinators. The cast from the untyped `storage0`
      is sound because `Addressable` instances are unique per medium.
  entry #start   the index of the first readable element in the storage
  entry #limit   the index one past the last readable element
  entry #skip   consumes `count` elements of the region without materializing them
  entry #regionStable   whether earlier region ranges stay valid after later refills; `false` unless overridden
      True for a single fixed buffer which `refill` only extends and `skip` only advances
      (`Stream(value)`, `streamOf(expanse, …)`), so a fan-out may share ranges by reference
      rather than copying. A stream which reuses its buffer must never claim it.
  entry #close   releases the stream's resources, propagating upstream through the whole chain

entry .stream   `value.stream` or `iterator.stream`: an in-memory pull stream, fluently
    Two extensions: on any `Addressable` value, `Stream(value)`; on a `murmuration.Iterator`
    of chunks, `Stream(iterator)`. The value's type must name the medium exactly (`: Data`,
    not a branded `Data & Sized[n]`).

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import codepages.utf8Codepage

    val bytes: Data = t"hello".in[Data]
    bytes.stream.memoize                                // a copy of the bytes
    Iterator(t"a", t"b").stream.memoize                 // t"ab"
    ```

entry .Intake   the push endpoint of a pipeline: a buffer whose writable region is lent zero-copy to one producer
  detail
      An `Intake[medium] over transport` reports its current `#demand` — how much it can
      accept — and is written through the primitive protocol `#reserve`/`#unsafeBuffer`/
      `#mark`/`#commit`: a writer reserves contiguous space, writes directly into the
      storage at `mark`, then commits. `#put`, `#push` and `#absorb` are final loops over
      that protocol, and since `Intake` extends `Producer`, serializing code written against
      `put`/`push` drives a pipeline unchanged. `#finish` ends the stream (exactly once),
      `#flush` propagates buffered data without ending it, and `#fail` aborts, propagating
      the error to the reader at the next asynchronous boundary.

      An intake is transformed into a differently-typed one with `accepting`/`acceptingDuct`,
      fed a whole stream with `pump` (which consumes it) or `ingest` (which hands it back),
      and built by `Conduit` for the writing thread of a cross-thread pipeline. Implementing
      one means providing `demand`, `buffer0`, `mark`, `reserve`, `commit` and `finish`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      Conduit[Data]() match
        case (intake, stream) =>
          intake.put(t"abc".in[Data])
          intake.finish()
          stream.memoize   // the three bytes
      ```
  refer zephyrine.Stream
  refer zephyrine.Producer
  refer zephyrine.Conduit
  refer zephyrine.accepting
  entry #addressable   the medium's `Addressable` instance
  entry #Operand   the element type: `addressable.Operand`
  entry #Transport   the demand type
  entry #demand   the demand this intake currently reports: advisory for bounding upstream production
      Computed on request and never cached; `reserve` is what actually blocks.
  entry #reserve   ensures contiguous writable space of at least one element, blocking if necessary, and returns how much
      `min` is a request: an implementation may return less (writers loop over
      `reserve`/`commit`), but may also take a large request as a sizing hint — a `Conduit`
      mints a correspondingly larger transfer block.
  entry #unsafeBuffer   the storage, writable from `mark` up to the reserved space, behind `Unsafe`
      Valid only until the next `commit`, `flush` or `finish`.
  entry #mark   the index at which the next element is written
  entry #commit   declares `count` elements written at `mark`; may cascade them synchronously downstream
  entry #flush   propagates buffered data downstream now, without ending the stream
  entry #finish   ends the stream: flushes, emits any terminal stage state, releases downstream; called once
  entry #fail   aborts: propagates the error downstream so stages release resources; it is rethrown to the reader
  entry #absorb   copies `count` elements of a storage, from an offset, through the reserve/commit loop
  entry #put   writes a whole value, or `size` elements of it from an ordinal, through the reserve/commit loop
      Overridable: an asynchronous boundary passes a large immutable chunk through by
      reference instead, as `Conduit`'s intake does.
  entry #push   writes one element

entry .Conduit   the asynchronous boundary: an intake for the writing thread and a stream for the reading thread
  detail
      `Conduit[medium]()` yields `(intake, stream)`, with exactly one thread ever touching
      each. Data crosses in blocks through a bounded lock-free ring (`Handoff`), so a writer
      which outpaces its reader parks in `reserve` — cross-thread backpressure — and the free
      capacity is the credit the intake reports as `demand`. It is the only synchronized
      component of a pipeline; every other stage is single-owner state on one side or the
      other. A chunk at least a block long whose medium exposes its backing (`Data`) crosses
      by reference, with no copy; smaller writes coalesce into transfer blocks, which the
      reader returns to the writer for reuse (`Buffering#recycle`), and then to the shared
      `Blockpool` when the conduit ends. A producer's `fail(error)` is rethrown to the reader
      at its next `refill`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import supervisors.globalSupervisor
      import probates.panicProbate
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val payload: Data = t"a long body".in[Data]

      supervise:
        Conduit[Data]() match
          case (intake, stream) =>
            val reader = stream.transfer: (stream, _, _) ?=>
              stream().memoize

            payload.stream.pump(intake)
            reader.await()   // the payload, read on the other fiber
      ```

      Threading decides throughput at this boundary: a handful of pipelines on idle cores
      run fastest on platform threads, whose spin budget catches most exchanges without a
      kernel transition, while under heavy load — more pipelines than cores — both endpoints
      should run on virtual threads, so every park suspends fiber-style on the carrier pool.
      Needs a `Buffering` (for the block size, the transfer ceiling, the depth and the
      recycling policy).
  refer zephyrine.Handoff
  refer zephyrine.Blockpool
  refer zephyrine.Buffering
  entry .apply   a fresh writer intake and reader stream over a shared ring, as a pair

entry .Duct   a synchronous transformation stage, attachable to a stream with `viaDuct` or an intake with `acceptingDuct`
  detail
      A `Duct[operand, result]` is a buffer-to-buffer kernel: `#step` transforms the readable
      range of a source `Region` into the writable space of a target `Slate`, returning how
      much it consumed and produced; `#flush` emits terminal state once the upstream ends;
      `#translate` converts downstream demand (in `result` elements) into the demand to
      present upstream (in `operand` elements). The same instance serves both directions of
      attachment, since demand is translated whichever way it is reported.

      The contract `step` must keep: given at least one input element and at least
      `#quantum` elements of output space, it consumes or produces at least one element;
      input it cannot yet transform (a partial atom at the end of the offered input) is
      consumed and carried in the duct's own state, never left unconsumed across steps. A
      duct whose state can retain output beyond one step's space (a decompressor) must
      override `flush`, which is called repeatedly until it returns `0`. `translate` must not
      starve — whenever `demand` grants at least `quantum`, the translation grants at least
      one — and must handle unbounded credit without overflow (ceiling divisions as
      `n - n/2`). Conversions are conservative bounds, not exact arithmetic: surplus is
      retained for the next transfer, which is what makes ducts with unknowable ratios
      (compression) possible.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      // Doubles each byte: 1024 of downstream demand is 512 upstream.
      class Doubler() extends Duct[Data, Data]:
        type Transport = Credit
        type Upstream = Credit

        def regulation: Credit is Regulation = summon[Credit is Regulation]
        def translate(demand: Credit): Credit = Credit(demand.count - demand.count/2)
        override def quantum: Int = 2

        def step(source: Region[Data])(range: Interval in source.type)
          (target: Slate[Data])(space: Interval in target.type)
        :   Duct.Progress =

          val count = (range: Interval).size.min((space: Interval).size/2)
          var written = 0

          source.visit(range.capped(count)): index =>
            val byte = source(index)
            target.borrow(space): (out, offset, _) =>
              out(offset + written) = byte
              out(offset + written + 1) = byte
            written += 2

          Duct.Progress(count, written)

      val doubled: Data = Duct.feed(t"ab".in[Data], Doubler())   // the bytes of "aabb"
      ```

      A duct is single-owner mutable state (compressors, partial atoms), so it is a stateful
      capability: `step`, `flush` and `close` are `update` methods needing an exclusive
      reference, while `translate`, `quantum` and `regulation` are pure queries. Its two
      `Addressable` instances are plain members, `#input` and `#output`, not givens, so that
      a same-medium duct does not see two ambiguous instances. A kernel wrapping an
      offset-based JDK API (a charset coder, a cipher, a compressor) widens the branded
      intervals back to offsets at its boundary through `Region.borrow`/`Slate.borrow`, or
      `unsafeRaw` behind `Unsafe`.
  refer zephyrine.Ductile
  refer zephyrine.Region
  refer zephyrine.Slate
  refer zephyrine.viaDuct
  entry .Progress   the unboxed (consumed, produced) pair `step` returns
    detail
        An opaque `Long` packing two `Int`s, so a step allocates nothing: `Duct.Progress(consumed,
        produced)` builds one, `consumed` and `produced` read it back.
    entry .apply   a progress of `consumed` input elements and `produced` output elements
    entry .consumed   how many input elements the step consumed
    entry .produced   how many output elements the step produced
  entry .feed   drives a duct over a whole value, as if it were the single region of a stream
      `Duct.feed(value, duct)` runs `step` over the value's storage (its backing directly when
      the medium exposes one, else one copy in) until the input is consumed, accumulating
      output into the medium's builder, then `flush`es and `close`s — the whole-value
      counterpart of `stream.viaDuct(duct)`, with no endpoint, no credit accounting and no
      refill cycle. Needs a `Buffering`, for the output window. This is how `compress[Gzip]`
      and `encode[Base64]` run a streaming kernel over a value in hand.
  entry #input   the operand medium's `Addressable` instance
  entry #output   the result medium's `Addressable` instance
  entry #Transport   the downstream demand type
  entry #Upstream   the demand type presented upstream
  entry #regulation   the `Regulation` of the downstream demand type
  entry #translate   converts downstream demand into the demand this duct presents upstream
  entry #quantum   the minimum output space `step` needs to guarantee progress; `1` by default
      `2` for a duct whose smallest output atom is two elements (a hex encoder, a UTF-8
      decoder emitting a surrogate pair).
  entry #sizing   the output window a streaming driver allocates for this duct; the staging block by default
      A duct whose kernel pays a large fixed cost per call (a JNI crossing) and expands its
      input (decompression) overrides this to the transfer block, so the fixed cost is
      amortised over sixteen times the output.
  entry #step   transforms the readable range of a source region into the writable space of a target slate
  entry #flush   emits terminal state after the upstream ends; called repeatedly until it returns `0`
      The default returns `0`. A duct which can retain undelivered output beyond one step
      (a decompressor) must override it: the first `0` is taken as the end of the stream.
  entry #close   releases the duct's resources, once the pipeline is done with it

entry .Ductile   makes a stage descriptor — a charset, a codepage, a compression scheme — attachable as a duct
  detail
      `stage is Ductile by in to out over transport` says the value describes a stage, and
      `duct(stage)` builds the `Duct` for it, with `Upstream` (the demand type presented
      upstream, `Credit` in almost every case) as a further member. `stream.via(stage)` and
      `intake.accepting(stage)` summon it, so a `Charset`, a `Codepage`, a `Gzip` or a cipher
      mode attaches directly, while a `Duct` itself — already a stage, not a description of
      one — attaches with `viaDuct`/`acceptingDuct` and has no instance. The companion holds
      `.charset` (bytes to text) and `.codepage` (text to bytes); compression and
      cryptography libraries supply their own. An instance is not `Typeclass.Pure`: it may
      capture what its ducts raise through, so it is passed as capturing evidence.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      val text: Text = t"résumé"
      val bytes: Data = text.stream.via(summon[Codepage]).memoize
      bytes.stream.via(summon[Charset]).memoize == text   // true
      ```
  refer zephyrine.Duct
  refer zephyrine.via
  refer zephyrine.accepting
  entry .Instance   the fully-determined instance type, naming all five members: `Instance[stage, in, out, transport, upstream]`
  entry .charset   character decoding as a stage: `Charset is Ductile by Data to Text over Credit`
      Bytes are staged internally so a multi-byte character split across refills is carried,
      and malformed input is substituted through the charset's `TextSanitizer`, exactly as
      whole-value decoding does. UTF-8 takes a hand-rolled kernel — ASCII runs widen eight
      bytes at a time, multi-byte sequences decode inline with strict validity — and falls
      back to the JDK decoder only for malformed input. An astral character emits a
      surrogate pair, so the duct's quantum is `2`.
  entry .codepage   character encoding as a stage: `Codepage is Ductile by Text to Data over Credit`
      A split surrogate pair at end-of-stream and an unmappable character are replaced,
      mirroring whole-value encoding. Demand translates by the encoder's worst-case bytes
      per char, floored at one.
  entry #Transport   the downstream demand type of the duct built
  entry #Upstream   the demand type the duct presents upstream
  entry #duct   builds the duct for a stage, consuming the stage; needs a `Buffering`

entry .Regulation   the arithmetic of a demand type: how much it grants, how it is spent, how it packs into a `Long`
  detail
      Demand values do not travel as messages: a stage computes its demand on request by
      converting its downstream's (`Duct#translate`), bottoming out in shared state at an
      asynchronous boundary. `encode`/`decode` pack every demand type into a `Long`, so that
      shared state is one atomic value whatever the type. The companion holds `.credit` and
      `.pace`.
  entry .credit   `Credit is Regulation`: unbounded initially, granting the count clamped to `0..Int.MaxValue`
  entry .pace   `Pace is Regulation`: `Halted` grants nothing, the others everything; `Measured` asks the pump to pace itself
  entry #initial   the demand before any is reported
  entry #grant   how many elements a demand grants, as an `Int`
  entry #spend   the demand after `count` elements have been produced against it
  entry #measured   whether the demand asks the pump to pace itself between blocks
  entry #encode   the demand packed into a `Long`
  entry #decode   a demand unpacked from a `Long`

entry .Pace   coarse backpressure demand: `Free`, `Measured` or `Halted`
    For a sink which cannot usefully count elements: `Free` asks the upstream to send
    freely, `Measured` to pace itself between blocks, and `Halted` to stop producing until
    demand is polled again. `Regulation.pace` is its arithmetic.

entry .Records   a stream of parsed records: `Stream[Array[record]^{}] over Credit`
    Each region is a frozen-array chunk of records, so credit counts records and `Buffering`
    sizes buffers by reference count. Records are immutable values crossing stage and thread
    boundaries by reference (a record must not itself hold a live endpoint); the record
    type must erase to a reference type, and the `Addressable` given which admits it
    (`Addressable.boxed`, `texts` or `frames`) governs at stream construction. `records`
    iterates the records of such a stream, and `memoize` materializes them into one frozen
    array.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Row(id: Int)
    val rows: Records[Row] = Stream(Iterator(Array(Row(1), Row(2)), Array(Row(3))))
    rows.records.to[List]   // List(Row(1), Row(2), Row(3))
    ```

entry .via   `stream.via(stage)`: a differently-typed stream pulling through a stage with a `Ductile` instance
  detail
      Pull-composition: the result's refills translate demand through the stage and pull from
      the consumed upstream, entirely on the consumer's thread. The stage may be any value
      with a `stage is Ductile by in` instance whose `Upstream` is the stream's transport: a
      `Charset`, a `Codepage`, a compression scheme, a cipher mode. Needs a `Buffering`. A
      stage which translates a productive demand into one granting nothing is a contract
      violation and panics rather than livelocking.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import charsets.utf8Charset

      val bytes: Data = t"The quick brown fox".in[Data]
      bytes.stream.via(summon[Charset]).memoize   // t"The quick brown fox"
      ```

      The forwarder in the `soundness` umbrella is hand-written, since a synthesized export
      forwarder loses the dependent `ductile.Result` refinement under capture checking; it
      behaves identically.
  refer zephyrine.Ductile
  refer zephyrine.viaDuct

entry .viaDuct   `stream.viaDuct(duct)`: a differently-typed stream pulling through a `Duct` directly
  detail
      A `Duct` is a stage, not a description of one, so it needs no `Ductile` instance: the
      duct type already states its operand, result and both transports. The duct and the
      stream are both consumed; closing the result closes both. Going through `Ductile`
      instead would mean instantiating it at the duct's own type, which capture checking
      cannot express. With a `Duct[Data, Data]` named `Doubler`,
      `bytes.stream.viaDuct(Doubler()).memoize` is the doubled bytes.
  refer zephyrine.Duct

entry .pump   `stream.pump(intake)`: drains a pull chain into a push chain on the calling thread
  detail
      The pump loop polls the intake's demand, refills the stream with it, and transfers the
      region into the intake; it is the only place data crosses from the pull side to the
      push side of a pipeline. Both endpoints are consumed, and the stream is closed when
      the pump ends, so what the intake gathered must be read some other way — or through
      `ingest`, which hands the intake back. A pump into a full `Conduit` parks until the
      reader drains it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      Conduit[Data]() match
        case (intake, stream) =>
          t"payload".in[Data].stream.pump(intake)
          stream.memoize   // the bytes of "payload"
      ```
  refer zephyrine.ingest

entry .ingest   `intake.ingest(stream)`: takes in a whole stream and returns the intake, typed as the caller's own class
  detail
      As `stream.pump(intake)`, but the intake is handed back once the stream is exhausted
      and the intake finished, so what it gathered can be read through the one returned
      reference — `Gather().ingest(bytes.stream).data` — with no second reference ever
      existing. The stream is consumed and closed.

entry .accepting   `intake.accepting(stage)`: a differently-typed intake pushing through a stage with a `Ductile` instance
  detail
      Push-composition: the result reports translated demand, and its commits step
      synchronously through the stage into the consumed intake's writable region. The same
      stage value serves `via` and `accepting`; only the attachment differs. Needs a
      `Buffering`. The umbrella forwarder is hand-written for the reason `via`'s is.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      Conduit[Data]() match
        case (intake, stream) =>
          val text = intake.accepting(summon[Codepage])
          text.put(t"résumé")
          text.finish()
          stream.memoize   // the UTF-8 bytes
      ```
  refer zephyrine.Ductile
  refer zephyrine.acceptingDuct

entry .acceptingDuct   `intake.acceptingDuct(duct)`: a differently-typed intake pushing through a `Duct` directly
  detail
      As `accepting`, for a `Duct` in hand rather than a descriptor. The duct's `flush` runs
      when the result is finished, and its `close` on finish or failure.

entry .drain   `stream.drain(operation)`: a terminal applying an operation to each successive region and its branded interval
  detail
      Drains and closes the stream, lending each region to `operation` as a `Region[medium]`
      with an `Interval in region.type`; the operation must not retain the region beyond the
      call. Each refill asks for a transfer block of credit, so a larger region is not sliced
      into many partial refills. Needs a `Buffering`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      var count = 0

      Stream(Iterator(t"ab", t"cde")).drain: region =>
        range => region.visit(range) { index => count += 1 }

      count   // 5
      ```
  refer zephyrine.gather
  refer zephyrine.Region

entry .memoize   `stream.memoize`: drains the stream into one immutable value of its medium
  detail
      The explicit, bounded replacement for a lazy list's implicit caching: the stream is
      drained once and closed, and the result is frozen and freely shareable. Each region is
      copied once at its exact size, and the pieces joined once at the end
      (`Addressable#assemble`), so a value delivered in one region is copied once, and any
      other twice. An empty stream yields the medium's `empty`. For a record stream the
      result is one frozen array of every record. Needs a `Buffering`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Stream(Iterator(t"ab", t"cd", t"e")).memoize   // t"abcde"
      ```

entry .gather   `stream.gather(initial)(operation)`: a terminal threading an accumulator through each region
  detail
      The accumulating counterpart of `drain`: the operation receives the running state with
      each region and its branded interval, and the final state is returned once the stream
      is drained and closed. Unlike an element-wise fold over a collection it exposes the
      whole region, so a byte reduction runs over the array with no per-element boxing.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val bytes: Data = Data.fill(100)(_.toByte)

      bytes.stream.gather(0L): region =>
        (sum, range) =>
          var total = sum
          region.visit(range) { index => total += region(index) }
          total
      ```

entry .truncate   `stream.truncate(count)`: the first `count` elements, then end-of-stream
    The remainder of the upstream is released unread on `close`. Counts elements of the
    medium — bytes, chars or records — not collection elements, which is why it does not
    borrow the name `take`. Composes with `discard` into a slice, and with `via` into a
    bounded pipeline.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val bytes: Data = Data.fill(20)(_.toByte)
    bytes.stream.discard(5).truncate(5).memoize   // bytes 5 to 9
    ```

entry .discard   `stream.discard(count)`: skips the first `count` elements, then passes the rest through unchanged
    The skipped elements are pulled and discarded on the first `refill`. Counts elements of
    the medium, not collection elements, which is why it is not called `drop`.

entry .chunks   `stream.chunks`: a single-consumer iterator of materialized chunks, one per refill
    The bridge by which stream content interleaves with other values (archive payloads
    between headers, parts between boundaries) without materializing the whole stream. The
    iterator owns the endpoint: it closes the stream when it reports exhaustion, so a
    consumer must drain it, or close the stream by other means, to release the upstream.
    Needs a `Buffering`.

entry .chain   `stream.chain`: a lazy `Chain` of one materialized chunk per refill, strictly demand-driven
    Construction pulls nothing and each forced cell pulls at most one refill. This is the
    audited bridge for consumers not yet converted to the kernel: a `Chain` is pure, so it
    cannot carry the endpoint capability its cells close over, and beyond this point the
    ownership discipline is suspended and every forced chunk is retained. Prefer the kernel
    terminals (`memoize`, `drain`, `gather`, `chunks`).

entry .records   `stream.records`: an iterator over the records of a `Records[record]` stream, in order
    Element-wise access to a record stream, across its regions. The iterator owns the
    endpoint and closes the stream when it reports exhaustion. Per-record combinators come
    from the `Iterator` interface; region-level access for hot loops is `drain`/`gather`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Row(id: Int)
    Stream(Iterator(Array(Row(1), Row(2)), Array(Row(3)))).truncate(2).records.to[List]   // List(Row(1), Row(2))
    ```

entry .streamOf   a stream lending part of a cursor — by length or up to a delimiter — or a range of an `Expanse`
  detail
      Three factories, each yielding a `Stream[…] over Credit`:

      - `streamOf(cursor, length)` — the next `length` elements of a cursor (or all remaining,
        if `Unset`), exposed zero-copy from the cursor's own buffer; skipping the stream
        advances the cursor. The cursor is **lent**, not consumed: closing the stream leaves
        it open, positioned at the boundary, for the caller to resume. This is the shape of
        a delimited payload inside a longer parse — an HTTP body before the next pipelined
        request, an archive entry before the next header. While the lent stream is live the
        caller must not touch the cursor, and bulk skips bypass lineation, so lend only
        cursors whose lineation is inactive.
      - `streamOf(cursor, delimiter)` — the bytes of a `Cursor[Data]` before the next
        occurrence of a `Cursor.Delimiter`, found in bulk with `distance`. Bytes which might
        begin a delimiter straddling the next fill are carried in a private scratch and
        searched joined with the next region. At end-of-stream the cursor stands after the
        delimiter, or is exhausted if the input ended first; the caller tells the two apart
        with `finished`.
      - `streamOf(expanse, offset, length)` — a range of an `Expanse`, read a transfer block
        at a time as the consumer demands, so an unconsumed payload costs nothing and one of
        any length streams in bounded memory. Its regions are stable (each refill installs a
        fresh immutable chunk). Needs a `Buffering`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val cursor = Cursor(Data.fill(10)(_.toByte): Data)
      val head: Data = streamOf(cursor, 4).memoize       // bytes 0 to 3
      cursor.remainder.to[List]                          // one chunk: bytes 4 to 9
      ```
  refer zephyrine.Cursor
  refer zephyrine.Expanse

entry .Producer   the sink a serializer writes into, in pieces: driven synchronously, as a channel of blocks, or as a push sink
  detail
      A `Producer[medium]` takes `#put(value)`, `#put(value, offset, size)` and `#push(element)`,
      where `Operand` is `Char` for `Producer[Text]` and `Byte` for `Producer[Data]`. The
      same producing code runs three ways without duplication: `.collect` runs it
      synchronously into a builder and returns the whole value (`show` and `encode`);
      `.apply` makes a `Channel` which stages blocks into a bounded queue drained through
      `Channel#iterator` by another fiber (a streamed HTTP body); and `.sink` hands each
      block to a consumer inline on the calling thread (a file, a socket, an `OutputStream`).
      `.utf8` and `.lendUtf8` are text producers whose blocks are delivered as UTF-8 bytes,
      encoded as the characters arrive, and `.Emission` chooses between `sink` and `utf8` by
      the medium a format's push form is asked for. None of them allocates per `put` along a
      contiguous run. An `Intake` is a `Producer`, so code written against one drives a
      pipeline unchanged.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      val csv: Text = Producer.collect[Text](): producer =>
        producer.put(t"name,count")
        producer.push('\n')
        producer.put(t"apples,3")

      val encoded: Data = Producer.collect[Data](): producer =>
        producer.push(0x01.toByte)
        producer.put(t"payload".in[Data])
      ```

      A producer is a stateful capability: writing needs the exclusive `Producer[…]^` the
      factories hand to the body, and the write methods are `update` methods. The byte-level
      `.Utf8Writer` is the lower-level writer a text format's push form uses to encode and
      escape straight into leased byte blocks, so a document is never built as a `Text` and
      encoded again.
  refer zephyrine.Intake
  refer zephyrine.Addressable
  refer zephyrine.Buffering
  entry .Bytes   `Producer[Data]` with its element pinned to `Byte`: the shape binary encoders write into
  entry .apply   a streaming producer: blocks are queued with backpressure and drained through its iterator
      `Producer[medium](block, depth)` makes a `Channel`: `put`/`push` fill a staging block
      of `block` elements (the `Buffering`'s capacity for the medium by default), each full
      block is queued, and `Channel#iterator` drains the queue. `put` blocks once the queue
      holds `depth` blocks (2 by default), so the producing code must run on a separate
      fiber from the consumer; `finish()` flushes the partial last block and ends the
      iterator.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import supervisors.globalSupervisor
      import probates.panicProbate
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      supervise:
        val producer = Producer[Text](4, 2)
        val reader = producer.iterator

        producer.transfer: (producer, _, _) ?=>
          producer().put(t"12345678901")
          producer().finish()

        reader.to[List]   // List(t"1234", t"5678", t"901")
      ```
  entry .collect   runs a body against a producer synchronously and returns the whole value
      `Producer.collect[Text](hint)(body)`: no concurrency, no chunk buffer, none of the
      streaming path's single-thread deadlock risk. The hint sizes the initial builder, 256
      by default, since the builder doubles as it fills and a large start is paid in full by
      every small output.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Producer.collect[Text](): producer =>
        producer.put(t"--hello--", 2.z, 5)   // t"hello"
      ```
  entry .sink   runs a body against a producer which hands each filled block to a consumer, inline
      `Producer.sink[medium](deliver, block)(body)`: the synchronous streaming form, the
      model of a generator writing to an `OutputStream`. Each staging block is handed to
      `deliver` on the calling thread as it fills, so the consumer sees the output in pieces
      before it is complete, with no fiber and no queue; the partial last block is flushed
      once `body` returns. A pull consumer (an iterator, an HTTP body) needs `.apply`'s
      channel instead. Needs a `Buffering`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      var blocks: List[Text] = Nil

      Producer.sink[Text](text => blocks ::= text, 4): producer =>
        producer.put(t"abcdefgh")

      blocks.reverse   // List(t"abcd", t"efgh")
      ```
  entry .Staged   the staging shared by the streaming producers: a block of elements, published when full
    detail
        An abstract class over `Channel` and `Sink`: writes fill a block of `block` elements,
        and each full block (and, at `finish`, the partial last one) is materialized and
        handed to the protected `publishChunk`, which a subclass implements as a queue put or
        a consumer call. Not normally extended by users; `.apply` and `.sink` build the two
        subclasses.
    entry #addressable   the medium's `Addressable` instance
    entry #put   writes a value, or `size` elements of it from an ordinal, through the staging block
    entry #push   writes one element
    entry #finish   publishes the partial last block
  entry .Channel   the streaming producer `Producer.apply` builds: blocks queued for an iterator on another fiber
    detail
        `Channel[medium, operand]` queues each full block in a bounded `ArrayBlockingQueue`
        of `depth` blocks, parking the producer when it is full; `#iterator` is the reader's
        view, a fresh iterator per call over the queue alone, so it outlives the producer's
        transfer to its fiber. `#finish` queues the end marker.
    entry #iterator   the reader-side view: an iterator of the queued blocks, ending after `finish`
        A `def`, minting a fresh iterator over the queue each time it is called; it is owned
        by the consuming thread and takes blocks as they are published, parking until one
        arrives or the producer finishes.
    entry #finish   publishes the partial last block and ends the iterator
  entry .Sink   the push producer `Producer.sink` builds: each full block handed to a consumer inline
  entry .Lending   the type of a consumer lent a filled block: `(region: Region[medium]) => (Interval in region.type) => Unit`
      The block's storage is lent as a `Region` with its branded extent, valid only for the
      duration of the call (the discipline of `Stream#lend`), so nothing is copied; a
      consumer which must keep the bytes materializes them itself with `region.materialize(range)`.
  entry .lendUtf8   a text producer whose blocks are lent as UTF-8 byte regions, encoded as characters arrive
      `Producer.lendUtf8(lending, block)(body)`: characters are encoded straight into a byte
      block of `block` bytes (the `Buffering`'s byte capacity by default), and each full block
      is lent to `lending` inline, so a text serializer writes bytes to a socket without
      materializing a `Text` per block and encoding it again, and without copying the block.
      A lone surrogate encodes as U+FFFD, as `String.getBytes` would. The byte block and the
      char scratch are leased from the `Blockpool` and returned when the body finishes.
  entry .utf8   a text producer whose blocks are delivered as fresh UTF-8 `Data` the consumer may keep
      The owning form of `.lendUtf8`: each block is materialized and passed to `deliver`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      var blocks: List[Data] = Nil

      Producer.utf8(data => blocks ::= data): producer =>
        producer.put(t"résumé")

      blocks.map(_.length)   // List(8): six characters, two of them two bytes
      ```
  entry .Utf8Sink   the producer behind `lendUtf8` and `utf8`: a `Producer[Text]` encoding into a leased byte block
    detail
        Encoding goes through the JDK's UTF-8 `CharsetEncoder`, whose ASCII path is
        vectorized: characters are inflated into a reused scratch and encoded into the byte
        block, which is published through the `Lending` when full. A trailing high surrogate
        is kept between calls, and a malformed pair encodes as U+FFFD.
    entry #put   writes a text, or `size` characters of it from an ordinal
    entry #push   writes one character
    entry #finish   encodes the pending tail, publishes the last block, and returns the buffers to the pool
  entry .Utf8Writer   a byte-level writer for a text format's push form: UTF-8 and escaping in one pass into leased blocks
    detail
        `Utf8Writer(lending, block)` encodes UTF-8 straight into a byte block leased from the
        `Blockpool`, escaping each string through the format's table as it goes, and lends
        each filled block to `lending` as `lendUtf8` does. A serializer writing through it
        never builds a `Text` for its output, so nothing is encoded twice; it is the model
        behind JSON's, XML's and TEL's byte writers, shared so that each format supplies only
        its escapes. Written with `.lend`, which flushes when the body returns.

        Its methods, all `update` methods: `#ascii` for text known to be ASCII (markup);
        `#name` for a document's repeated names, through a cache of encoded names matched by
        identity or equality; `#text` for text with nothing escaped; `#escaped` for text with
        each ASCII character named in an `Escapes` table replaced by its entity; `#bytes` for
        bytes already encoded; `#byte` for one byte; `#long` for a decimal integer; and
        `#finish`. A lone surrogate encodes as U+FFFD, except in a `name`, where
        `String.getBytes` writes `?` (no well-formed name contains one).

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val escapes = Producer.Utf8Writer.escapes('<' -> "&lt;", '>' -> "&gt;", '&' -> "&amp;")
        var blocks: List[Data] = Nil

        Producer.Utf8Writer.lend(region => range => blocks ::= region.materialize(range)): writer =>
          writer.ascii("<p>")
          writer.escaped("a < b", escapes)
          writer.ascii("</p>")

        blocks   // one block: the bytes of "<p>a &lt; b</p>"
        ```
    entry .NameSlots   the number of slots in the writer's name cache: 256
    entry .Escapes   an escape table: for each ASCII character, the entity which replaces it, if any
      detail
          Built by `.escapes`: `codes` holds, for each ASCII character, zero when it is written
          as it is or the one-based number of its entity; `offsets` and `data` hold the
          entities' bytes. Pure and flat, so the encoding loop tests one byte per character.
      entry #codes   for each ASCII character, zero or the one-based entity number
      entry #offsets   the entity boundaries within `data`
      entry #data   the entities' bytes, concatenated
    entry .escapes   an escape table replacing each listed ASCII character with its entity
    entry .lend   writes with a body through a writer lending each block, then flushes it
        `Producer.Utf8Writer.lend(lending, block)(body)`; the block size defaults to the
        `Buffering`'s byte capacity.
    entry #byte   writes one byte
    entry #ascii   writes text known to be ASCII, one byte per character
    entry #bytes   writes `source(from until end)`, bytes already encoded as UTF-8
    entry #text   writes text as UTF-8 with nothing escaped
    entry #name   writes a name as UTF-8, through the cache of recently written names
    entry #escaped   writes text as UTF-8 with the characters an `Escapes` table names replaced by their entities
    entry #long   writes a decimal integer
    entry #finish   flushes the last block and returns the buffers to the pool; the writer is spent
  entry .Emission   the media a text serializer's push form can be delivered as: `Text` blocks or UTF-8 `Data` blocks
    detail
        A format's `emit[medium](value, deliver)` summons `medium is Emission`, so the
        serializer is written once against `Producer[Text]`: `.text` runs it through `.sink`,
        `.data` through `.utf8`.
    entry .text   delivers `Text` blocks, through `Producer.sink`
    entry .data   delivers UTF-8 `Data` blocks, through `Producer.utf8`
    entry #run   runs a body against a text producer, delivering blocks of the medium; needs a `Buffering`
  entry #Operand   the element type: `Char` for `Producer[Text]`, `Byte` for `Producer[Data]`
  entry #put   writes a whole value, or `size` elements of it from an ordinal
  entry #push   writes a single element

entry .Region   a bounds-safe fragment of a medium's storage: an opaque reference whose extent travels as a branded interval
  detail
      A `Region[medium]` grants no direct access — no length, no raw indexed read. Its valid
      extent is lent beside it as an `Interval in region.type`, and the only producers of
      branded ordinals are the trusted combinators here, so `apply` is total: no bounds check,
      no `Optional`. A region plus its extent is one reference and one `Long`, allocation-free,
      replacing the `(storage, offset, length)` triples the kernel once threaded through
      streams and ducts. Validity is temporal — until the lender's next refill or reuse —
      which the brand cannot express; that remains the single-owner discipline of
      `Stream#lend` and `Cursor#lend`. `Slate` is the writable counterpart.

      `Region` is not exported to the `soundness` umbrella (its dependent-typed inline
      extensions do not survive export forwarders): write `import zephyrine.Region`, or
      `import zephyrine.*`, where it is named. The accessors are extension methods in the
      companion, found through implicit scope, so `region(index)`, `region.visit(range)` and
      `region.materialize(range)` read as instance methods.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import zephyrine.Region

      val storage = Array.scratch[Byte](10)
      var visited = 0

      Region.over[Data, Unit](storage, 2, 5): region =>
        range => region.visit(range) { index => visited += 1 }

      visited   // 3: the window is 2 until 5
      ```

      Within a kernel, a brand is widened away (`val interval: Interval = range`) before the
      interval's own inline operations apply, since the inliner does not see through the
      refinement.
  refer zephyrine.Slate
  refer zephyrine.Stream
  entry .over   the trusted constructor: lends a region over a storage, with its extent clamped to the storage's true extent
      `Region.over[medium, result](storage, offset, limit)(lambda)`, needing the medium's
      `Addressable`: the region is bound to a stable name and the lambda receives it with its
      branded interval, `offset until limit` clamped into the storage, so the totality
      invariant is established by construction.
  entry .apply   the element at a branded ordinal: total, with no bounds check
  entry .visit   runs a lambda on each branded ordinal of a range, in order
  entry .visit8   runs a lambda on eight consecutive ordinals at a time, with a second for the tail
      The derived-index answer for unrolled hot loops: the `+1`..`+7` arithmetic is proven
      once here, and user code only ever sees branded ordinals.
  entry .materialize   a new value of the medium copied from a range
  entry .cloneTo   copies a range into a builder of the medium's `Target` type
  entry .transfer   copies as much of a range as fits a slate's space, returning the count
  entry .unsafeRaw   the raw storage, behind `Unsafe`, for kernels whose index arithmetic outruns the combinators
      Compressor back-references and the like: the caller takes back responsibility for
      bounds.
  entry .borrow   lends a byte region's raw array with the range's offset and length, for `(array, offset, length)` APIs
      An extension on `Region[Data]`: `OutputStream.write`, `ByteBuffer.wrap`,
      `Deflater.setInput` and a digest's `update` take a triple which is in range by
      construction, so no `Unsafe` is needed; the array is visible only for the call, which
      must neither retain it nor index outside the triple.

entry .Slate   the writable counterpart of `Region`: a duct's output storage, written through branded ordinals
  detail
      `Slate[medium]` has `update` in place of `apply`, with the same branding discipline:
      `slate(index) = element` writes at a branded ordinal, `visit` runs over a range, and
      `borrow` lends the raw byte array with a range's offset and length for the APIs which
      fill a triple (`Deflater.deflate`, `InputStream.read`). `Slate.over` lends one over
      exclusive storage. Like `Region`, it is not exported to the umbrella: `import
      zephyrine.Slate`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import zephyrine.Slate

      val target = Array.scratch[Byte](6)

      Slate.over[Data, Unit](target, 2, 5): slate =>
        space => slate.visit(space) { index => slate(index) = 7.toByte }

      (target(1), target(2), target(5))   // (0, 7, 0)
      ```
  refer zephyrine.Region
  refer zephyrine.Duct
  entry .over   lends a slate over exclusive storage, with its extent clamped to the storage's true extent
  entry .update   writes an element at a branded ordinal
  entry .visit   runs a lambda on each branded ordinal of a range, in order
  entry .unsafeRaw   the raw exclusive storage, behind `Unsafe`
  entry .borrow   lends a byte slate's raw array, exclusively, with the space's offset and length

entry .Utf8   strict UTF-8 over a byte buffer, for parsers which scan bytes and materialize text only for the slices they keep
  detail
      Decoding is strict, as `Ductile.charset`'s kernel is: overlong forms, encoded surrogates
      and values above U+10FFFF are malformed, never substituted, so a parser can reject bad
      input rather than silently carry U+FFFD into a document. An all-ASCII slice costs one
      array copy, through the JDK's Latin-1 `String` constructor. Not exported to the
      umbrella: reached as `zephyrine.Utf8`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val bytes: scala.Array[Byte] = scala.Array('a'.toByte, 0xc3.toByte, 0xa9.toByte)
      zephyrine.Utf8.decode(bytes, 0, 3)             // t"aé"
      zephyrine.Utf8.point(bytes, 1, 3)              // 0xe9
      zephyrine.Utf8.decode(bytes, 0, 2)             // Unset: the sequence is cut short
      ```
  refer zephyrine.Words
  entry .Malformed   `point`'s result for an invalid sequence: `-1`
  entry .Incomplete   `point`'s result for a valid prefix cut short by the end: `-2`
      A caller with more input to come refills and retries.
  entry .width   the number of bytes in the sequence a lead byte begins: 1 to 4, or 0 for a byte which cannot lead
      A continuation byte, the overlong leads C0 and C1, and F5 to FF cannot lead a sequence.
  entry .continuation   whether a byte is a continuation byte, `10xxxxxx`
      Counting the bytes which are *not* continuations counts the code points in a range.
  entry .point   the code point of the sequence beginning at an offset and lying wholly before `end`, or `Malformed` or `Incomplete`
  entry .asciiEnd   the offset of the first non-ASCII byte in a range, or its end if there is none, scanning eight bytes per step
  entry .ascii   the text of a range the caller knows to be ASCII: one copy, no validation
  entry .decode   the text of a byte range, or `Unset` if it is not valid UTF-8
  entry .append   appends the text of a byte range to a `StringBuilder`, or appends nothing and returns `false` if it is invalid

entry .Words   SWAR primitives over a byte buffer: eight bytes read as one word and classified lane by lane
  detail
      `load` reads eight bytes as a little-endian `Long` (through the platform's `WordLoad`),
      and the masks classify each lane with the classic has-zero-byte arithmetic, so a scan
      for a stop byte advances eight bytes per step. Every mask has the high bit set in each
      lane which satisfies the test; the borrow from one flagged lane can spill into the lanes
      above it, so only the *lowest* flagged lane is exact — the one `first` reports, and the
      only one a scan needs. The tests are valid for ASCII lanes: a lane with its own high
      bit set is flagged by `nonAscii` and must be handled separately by a scan which cares.
      Not exported to the umbrella: reached as `zephyrine.Words`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import zephyrine.Words

      val bytes: scala.Array[Byte] = "abc<efgh".getBytes.nn
      val word = Words.load(bytes, 0)
      Words.first(Words.matches(word, Words.replicate('<')))   // 3
      ```
  refer zephyrine.WordLoad
  refer zephyrine.Utf8
  entry .HighBits   the high bit of every lane: `0x8080808080808080L`
  entry .EveryByte   one in every lane: `0x0101010101010101L`
  entry .load   eight bytes from an index, as a little-endian `Long`
  entry .replicate   a byte value replicated into all eight lanes, the comparand for `matches` and `below`
  entry .zeroes   the lanes of a word which are zero
  entry .matches   the lanes of a word equal to a replicated byte
  entry .below   the lanes of a word below a replicated byte, both ASCII
  entry .nonAscii   the lanes of a word holding a non-ASCII byte
  entry .first   the offset within the word of the lowest flagged lane of a non-zero mask

entry .WordLoad   the platform's little-endian 64-bit read over a byte buffer, behind `Words.load`
  detail
      Little-endian regardless of the platform, so `Words.first(mask)` is
      `numberOfTrailingZeros(mask) >> 3` everywhere. The JVM variant (`core-jvm`) reads the
      word through a byte-array view `VarHandle`; the Scala.js and Scala Native variant
      (`core-portable`) assembles it by shift-and-or, since neither has `java.lang.invoke`.
      Not exported to the umbrella.
  entry .get   eight bytes from an index, as a little-endian `Long`

entry .Blockpool   a process-wide, bounded pool of spent transfer blocks, shared across conduits and writers
  detail
      The second-level cache behind each `Conduit`'s `Freelist`: the freelist recycles blocks
      between one writer and reader over a conduit's lifetime, and this pool carries them
      *across* lifetimes, so a short-lived conduit, `Utf8Sink` or `Utf8Writer` mints its
      blocks from its predecessors' instead of allocating afresh. Blocks are pooled by their
      storage class and exact physical size, each class bounded to about 16 MiB retained
      (floored at four blocks, capped at 256). Multi-producer and multi-consumer, both
      operations non-blocking and best-effort: a `poll` miss means the caller allocates, an
      `offer` over the bound discards. Contents are never read — a reused block is
      overwritten before publication. Not exported to the umbrella.
  refer zephyrine.Conduit
  refer zephyrine.Freelist
  entry .offer   caches a spent block of a class and exact physical size, or discards it if the class is at bound
      The block must be fully relinquished by its offerer.
  entry .poll   a pooled block of exactly this class and physical size, or `null`, in which case the caller allocates

entry .Handoff   a bounded single-producer, single-consumer hand-off ring: the exchange behind `Conduit`
  detail
      Exactly one thread calls the producer operations (`offer`, `finish`) and exactly one the
      consumer operations (`take`, `drain`, `close`). Both sides spin briefly before parking,
      so a producer and consumer in lockstep exchange items without kernel transitions; the
      unpark handshake is the Dekker pattern, so a wakeup is never missed. Interruption is
      cancellation: a parked side raises `InterruptedException`, as a blocking queue's would.
      Its guarantees come from volatile publication order, not aliasing analysis. Not
      exported to the umbrella; parasite's queues build on it too.
  refer zephyrine.Conduit
  entry #width   the ring's slot count: the largest burst a `drain` can move
  entry #size   approximate occupancy, for advisory demand calculations; never for synchronization
  entry #free   the approximate number of free slots
  entry #offer   producer side: spins then parks until a slot frees, unless the consumer has closed, when the item is discarded
  entry #finish   producer side: no more items will be offered; idempotent
  entry #drain   consumer side: adopts every buffered item into a caller-owned array in one step, blocking until at least one arrives
      Returns the number moved: zero only once the producer has finished and the ring is
      drained. One `head` publication and one producer unpark per burst, where a `take` loop
      pays one of each per item.
  entry #take   consumer side: the next item, or `null` once the producer has finished and the ring is drained
  entry #close   consumer side: stops accepting, drains buffered items (releasing a parked producer) and discards later offers

entry .Freelist   a bounded single-producer, single-consumer pool of spent blocks: a conduit's return path
  detail
      Lets a `Conduit`'s reader hand a drained block back to its writer for reuse instead of
      dropping it to the collector while the writer mints a fresh one. Exactly one thread
      offers (the reader) and one polls (the writer), and neither ever blocks: this is a
      best-effort cache, not a rendezvous, so the writer can never stall on an empty pool —
      which would deadlock against a reader parked waiting for that same writer. Not exported
      to the umbrella.
  refer zephyrine.Blockpool
  entry #offer   caches a spent block, returning whether it was accepted; a full pool declines
  entry #poll   a cached block to reuse, or `null` if the pool is empty

entry .internal   the home of the `Credit` and `Datum` opaque types and the `consume` macro
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `Credit` and `Datum` are exported from here to the `zephyrine`
      package, and `consume` is the macro behind `Cursor#consume`.
  entry .Credit   the demand type; see `zephyrine.Credit`
    entry .apply   a credit of `count` elements
    entry .count   the number of elements the credit grants, as a `Long`
  entry .Datum   the peeked element type; see `zephyrine.Datum`
    entry .End   the sentinel for an exhausted cursor
    entry .apply   lifts an unsigned byte, or a char, into a `Datum`
    entry .datumByte   `Datum == Byte` comparison
    entry .byteDatum   `Byte == Datum` comparison
    entry .datumChar   `Datum == Char` comparison
    entry .charDatum   `Char == Datum` comparison
    entry .datumDatum   `Datum == Datum` comparison
    entry .asInt   the underlying `Int`; anywhere it leaks, the distinction is lost
    entry .isEnd   whether the cursor which produced this datum was exhausted
  entry .consume   the macro behind `Cursor#consume`: one `next()` and comparison per character of the literal
